  "text": "Let's focus on the function:\n\n```python\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return lst\n    \n    new_list = [lst[0]]\n    for element in lst[1:]:\n        if element != new_list[-1]:\n            new_list.append(element)\n    \n    return new_list\n```\n\nThe function `remove_adjacent_duplicates` works by iterating through the list and checking if the current element is different from the last one in the new list. If it is, it appends the current element to the new list, otherwise it skips it.\n\nTesting for `remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4])`:\n- The first element is `1`, which is added to the new list.\n- The next elements are `2`, `2`, `3`, `3`, `3`, and `4`.\n- For each of these elements, they are compared with the last element in the new list.\n- When `2` is compared to the last element `1`, it's added to the new list. Then the next `2` is compared to the new last element `2`, and since they are the same, it is skipped.\n- The same logic applies to the rest of the elements.\n- Finally, the new list becomes `[1, 2, 3, 4]`.\n\nThis logic is correct and aligns with the solution.",
